#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125899 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have a straight line passing through the point $P(3,-1,7)$ and that's parallel to the direction vector ![](images/54/54405c5c6e2502e5174525c2ee36a99366a79933b47e316a09858f827fda8dae.svg) As we know, we can write the vector equation of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nUsing the vector equation above, we can create a set of **parametric equations** where we express a general point $(x,y,z)$ in terms of  the parameter $t.$\n\nIf we let ![](images/67/67b8360f90540ffb75d39717c375f0ef6e9a8a39cae5832c78957105e0d148b6.svg) be the position vector of the general point $(x,y,z),$ then the above equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1+5t \\\\ 7-3t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n![](images/64/64cf325014eff341673557321ef565e35988b836630f3fbeb52af8024d7aaad7.svg)\n\nThe equations in the above system are called the **parametric equations** of the straight line.\nGeometrically, the equation states that a point belongs to the line if and only if its coordinates have the form\n\n$$\n(3, \\: -1+5t, \\: 7-3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n![](images/e2/e29ce95cdccb47b97578f6431a96e608f7af9f6709410b221ff517b436896608.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6ff73cc8-91a0-5a25-9c14-6e87496adac8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9660 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
